---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/reviews-and-ugc';
const title = 'Shopify Reviews and customer photos | ' + site.name;
const desc = 'Collect an honest product review after the customer can use the item, then reuse approved proof where it answers a buying question.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="container py-16"><a class="link" href="/use-cases">All workflow guides</a><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Reviews and customer photos</h1><p class="mt-7 text-xl text-cyan-100">Collect an honest product review after the customer can use the item, then reuse approved proof where it answers a buying question. Collection, support response and reuse permissions are separate jobs; one automation should not silently decide all three.</p></section>
<section class="container py-10"><div class="prose-shopify card"><h2>Eligibility starts with the delivered item</h2><p>Use the order line and delivery status to identify what the customer actually received. Fulfillment is not proof of delivery. Split shipments need item-level handling so the request does not ask about an unopened parcel. Where delivery data is absent, use a conservative estimated-arrival message and ask whether the item arrived before requesting product feedback.</p><p>A review already submitted should exit the request path for that item. A later purchase of the same product does not automatically justify another request. Track request and completion IDs to prevent both the review app and the email platform asking twice.</p></div></section>
<section class="container py-10"><div class="prose-shopify card"><h2>Timing follows useful experience</h2><p>For apparel, ask after a customer can assess fit; for a product requiring repeated use, explain what kind of experience is useful before asking. Choose timing from delivery and support data rather than copying a universal day count. Returns and delivery failures should route to service handling with a clear reason and re-entry rule.</p><p>Do not select reviewers based on predicted sentiment. A complaint may justify pausing a promotional campaign while service resolves it, but must not become a hidden filter that only invites satisfied customers to leave public reviews. Use the same honest-review invitation and destination for eligible purchasers.</p></div></section>
<section class="container py-10"><div class="prose-shopify card"><h2>Three proposed paths to implement</h2><p>Beauty: ask about texture or routine compatibility without suggesting a medical result. Include the purchased product and an accessible review link; a photo should be optional. Pet accessories: request fit and durability feedback, with guidance to avoid publishing personal details in photos. Home goods: account for installation before asking about usability. These are design examples, not observed merchant results.</p><p>Give the review program owner control of eligibility and cadence. Customer support owns damaged or missing items. The content owner approves quotes and photos for reuse, recording permission scope, source URL and withdrawal requests. An incentive, if offered, should reward participation without requiring a positive rating and needs appropriate disclosure.</p></div></section>
<section class="container py-10"><div class="prose-shopify card"><h2>Proof placement is a product matching problem</h2><p>Browse follow-up can answer a fit objection with a relevant customer quote. Cart content can address material or sizing uncertainty. A launch may have no verified customer reviews yet; use product details instead of implying that older reviews describe the new SKU. Replenishment often needs a reorder link more than another testimonial.</p><p>Check that the quote belongs to the advertised product, remains accurate and has permission for the intended channel. A screenshot of a public post is not a permission record. Do not rewrite a customer quote into a stronger claim. If a dynamic review source fails, omit the block or use approved evergreen copy rather than showing empty stars or a made-up count.</p></div></section>
<section class="container py-10"><div class="prose-shopify card"><h2>Tool evaluation: request events before galleries</h2><p>Start with the review service that holds the submission record. Ask it to demonstrate delivery-aware scheduling, submitted-review events, duplicate prevention and moderation rules. Then ask the messaging platform to consume the event for a thank-you or request exit. A visually attractive gallery does not establish that these events sync.</p><p>For Sequenzy, evaluate the Shopify connection and the specific review event transport separately. Its store integration does not establish a native connection to every review provider. For Klaviyo, its documented Shopify tags and segments can support audience rules, but review text and photo permissions still need their own integration. Keep the existing review service unless a demonstrated gap justifies replacing it.</p></div></section>
<section class="container py-10"><div class="prose-shopify card"><h2>Measure collection and reuse separately</h2><p>Collection rate is completed eligible reviews divided by eligible delivered requests, counted at product or order level consistently. Report delivery coverage, duplicate requests and time to support resolution alongside it. A high completion rate from a sentiment-filtered audience is not evidence of a healthier review program.</p><p>For reuse, compare an approved proof block with an otherwise equivalent message. Keep the offer, audience and measurement window stable; use purchase contribution and opt-outs as well as clicks. Record which review asset was shown so an outdated claim can be traced and removed. No conversion lift is assumed by this playbook.</p></div></section>
<section class="container py-10"><div class="prose-shopify card"><h2>Release and recurring ownership</h2><p>Before release, use test profiles for split delivery, prior review, missing tracking, a negative review and withdrawn photo permission. Check the actual destination on mobile. Confirm a completed review exits all reminder paths, including any reminder already queued by the review service.</p><p>At review time, inspect request duplication, unsupported product claims in reused copy and broken assets. Link this work to the post-purchase owner rather than creating a second uncoordinated customer calendar. Keep a record of why a customer was paused and when the pause should end.</p></div></section>
<section class="container py-10"><div class="prose-shopify card"><h2>Supplier scope and current sources</h2><p>Checked 25 November 2026. <a class="link" href="https://apps.shopify.com/sequenzy">Sequenzy’s Shopify listing</a> describes store synchronization and marketing automation. Its <a class="link" href="https://www.sequenzy.com/pricing">pricing page</a> separates send-based email plans from the paid SMS/MMS beta add-on. Confirm destination support and the complete quote for your intended workload. These supplier descriptions are not independent performance tests.</p><p>The <a class="link" href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115005251588">Klaviyo glossary</a> documents segments, Shopify tags and separate channel status. Validate the events, permissions and delays for your own configuration; the operational designs above do not claim every supplier implements every step natively.</p></div></section>
<section class="container py-10"><div class="prose-shopify card"><h2>Continue the operating plan</h2><p>Related guides: <a class="link" href="/use-cases/post-purchase">post purchase</a>, <a class="link" href="/use-cases/welcome-series">welcome series</a>. Compare the required behavior with <a class="link" href="/apps">app reviews</a> and <a class="link" href="/compare">platform comparisons</a>.</p></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
